Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 22:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 538/538] [DS4.1] The rank-order sum adds five and six ranks'
 parts

rank_order_sum_kernel declared a TileLang variant for two, three and four
sources only, so at TP5 and TP6 rank_order_sum fell back to torch (an FP32
copy of the first part, one add per other part: the same bits, several
passes and a full-size FP32 temporary for every prefill chunk past the
one-shot limit), and rank_order_sum_calls declared nothing.

The variants now share one body, sum_rows, whose loop over a Python tuple of
sources unrolls as the IR is built: source r is loaded r-th, the first taken
as is, every other added to the FP32 total, one rounding. Each rank count
still declares its own signature (TileLang reads a prim_func's parameters
from it), each source with its own T.Tensor. MAX_RANK_ORDER_WORLD (6) bounds
the kernel, rank_order_sum's dispatch and rank_order_sum_calls alike, so the
kernel the forward launches at TP5 and TP6 is the one kernel preparation
compiled.

Two to four sources build the same IR as before, statement for statement:
TileLang 0.1.15's get_tir gives identical scripts and structurally equal
PrimFuncs for worlds 2, 3 and 4 (host check, no GPU), so TP2-TP4 outputs
keep their bits. Five and six ranks exchange directly (exchange_into relays
only at four).

The host tests run the exchange, the reduce-scatter and the all-reduce at
five and six simulated ranks, and check the kernel's IR, in statement
order, for every rank count; the GPU tests add worlds 5 and 6 to the bit
test, a seven-part torch sum, and TP6's Engram and vocabulary shards to the
all-gather concatenation.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
